  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Before we solve Number System questions, we need to become comfortable with one simple idea:\n\nA digit does not always have the same value.\n\nLook at the digit 5:\n\nIn 5, it means five.\nIn 50, it means fifty.\nIn 500, it means five hundred.\n\nThe digit stayed the same.\nIts position changed.\n\nThat position is what gives a digit its place value.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "place_value_basics"
        }
      ]
    },
    "finish": {
      "id": "finish",
      "type": "COMPLETION",
      "content": {
        "text": "Great work.\n\nYou now have the four building blocks of this topic:\n\nFace value = the digit itself.\n\nPlace value = digit × the value of its position.\n\nExpanded form = split a number into the values of its digits.\n\nNumber formation:\nGreatest → arrange digits from largest to smallest.\nSmallest → smallest non-zero digit first, then the remaining digits in ascending order.\n\nOne final habit:\n\nAlways read positions from the right:\nones → tens → hundreds → thousands → ten-thousands...\n\nOnce that becomes automatic, many Number System questions become much easier.\n\nYou're ready for the next subtopic.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": []
    },
    "expanded_form": {
      "id": "expanded_form",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Place value also lets us break a number apart.\n\nTake:\n\n4,305\n\nThe digits represent:\n\n4 thousands = 4,000\n3 hundreds = 300\n0 tens = 0\n5 ones = 5\n\nSo the expanded form is:\n\n4,305 = 4,000 + 300 + 5\n\nWe usually leave the zero term out because adding zero changes nothing.\n\nThis is a useful way to check whether you have understood the position of every digit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "expanded_form_check"
        }
      ]
    },
