    "number_formation": {
      "id": "number_formation",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Now let's use place value in reverse.\n\nSuppose you are asked to form the greatest or smallest number from a set of digits.\n\nThere are two rules to remember.\n\nFor the greatest number:\nPut the largest available digit first, then arrange the rest in descending order.\n\nFor the smallest number:\nThe first digit cannot be zero.\nSo first choose the smallest non-zero digit.\nThen place zero next, followed by the remaining digits in ascending order.\n\nExample: digits 0, 2, 5, 7, using each digit once.\n\nGreatest:\n7 5 2 0 = 7,520\n\nSmallest:\n2 0 5 7 = 2,057\n\nWhy not 0 2 5 7?\n\nBecause 0257 is read as 257, not a four-digit number.\n\nThat leading-zero rule is one of the most common traps in number-formation questions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "greatest_number_check"
        }
      ]
    },
    "face_value_remedy": {
      "id": "face_value_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The word 'face' is the clue.\n\nFace value means:\n\nWhat digit is written there?\n\nIn 46,219, the digit is 6.\nSo its face value is simply 6.\n\nDo not multiply by 10, 100, or 1,000 unless the question asks for place value.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "face_value_retry"
        }
      ]
    },
